{"kind":"artifact","text":"spike at exact clean head a0f6b0055c533e7a1ab805f4bf6eabbdc0784480 (branch request/gitea-loopback-hardening), answering request e952d16d under promise b4b3706c. This artifact is the implementation report.\n\nThe defect, verified on main before I promised rather than taken from the description. compose.yaml published \"3300:3000\"; Docker reads a mapping with no host address as 0.0.0.0, so the development Gitea was offered on every network the host is attached to. On a fresh data volume that included the first-run installer, and whoever loads that page first becomes the administrator. GITEA__service__DISABLE_REGISTRATION was already set and does not cover it: it governs self-signup after installation, not installation.\n\nBoth guards are now in place: the published address is explicit loopback, and GITEA__security__INSTALL_LOCK closes first run.\n\nThe two changes had to travel together, which is the part worth checking rather than the port. Locking setup removes the browser path to the first administrator, so a compose file hardened alone would leave the guide's first documented step impossible - a worse outcome than the defect, because it fails at the moment someone is trying to use the lane rather than quietly. FORGE.md now states the trust boundary, creates that account with gitea admin user create, and carries a startup verification that refuses visibly if the port is not loopback or the installer is still reachable.\n\nThe regression is spike/forge, repository-native and needing no daemon, image or network, so it runs in the ordinary suite instead of behind the profile the lane is gated on. It parses the ports block rather than matching one known string, so a second service added later with the same mistake is caught; it fails when the lock is absent; and it fails when the guide loses the CLI step the lock makes necessary. Four mutations, each verified to have applied before the run, were each caught: reverting the mapping to \"3300:3000\", dropping the lock, publishing on 0.0.0.0, and removing the admin step from FORGE.md.\n\nThe boundary I did not cross, stated because the request asked me to determine the configuration rather than assume it. No Docker daemon is available in this environment - the CLI is present, the socket is not - so I could not pull 1.24.6 and confirm the key against the running image. What is established: the GITEA__section__KEY environment mechanism is confirmed by this file, which already configures three settings that way. What is not: that INSTALL_LOCK still carries this meaning at 1.24.6. The regression asserts the configuration is present, not that Gitea honours it. Certifying that needs the lane booted, which is exactly what the startup verification in the guide is for and why it is written to refuse rather than warn. A reviewer with a daemon can settle it in one command.\n\nGates at this exact head: go build, go vet, go test ./spike/... with all three packages passing, uncached make docs, git diff --check clean, worktree clean.\n\nScope held: no change to the documented workflow for private repositories and repo-scoped users beyond the administrator step the lock requires, and no change outside spike/.","body":{"commit":"a0f6b0055c533e7a1ab805f4bf6eabbdc0784480","path":"spike"}}